This suite tests fnmatch.

Each feature in the POSIX fnmatch pattern syntax is tested.

This suite is split into the strict base POSIX tests, which tests every sentence
that is mandatory in POSIX; and the nonstandard tests, which tests all the
optional behavior as well as common de facto extensions.

Each fnmatch feature is exhaustively combined with each other fnmatch feature to
ensure that the features work together. More complicated tests combine multiple
features in interesting cases. Each flag to fnmatch is tested as well with the
relevant features.

The POSIX requirements on fnmatch can be tricky to understand. The POSIX.1-2024
chapter 2.14 text could not be written to be more confusing, as well as the
relevant implications on the chapter 9 bracket expression syntax that is shared
with regular expressions, but with additional special rules. Many tests use a
truth table approach where every possible reasonable input is given to each
pattern, so it's more likely that implementation bugs are discovered.

This suite has resolved the proper behaviors of as many unthinkably weird edge
cases as possible. The backslash escaping rules have been an endless ride of
curious questions about what happens if you put backslashes in unexpected places
like [[:al\num:]], which the standard fails to consider explicitly, and we have
to interpret what other language does apply to this case. POSIX.1-2024 changed a
lot of the relevant language compared to the older POSIX.1-2008 in an effort to
fix contradictions, and the resulting prose is endlessly difficult to interpret,
and requires the rationale section to figure out what heck they even meant.
Some patterns have unspecified behavior and are not tested.

The nonstandard tests check every extension I know of, along with UTF-8 and
unicode support, including case sensitivity and the behavior on invalid UTF-8
sequences. It's technically fine to disagree with the nonstandard tests, and
they mostly exist to study the availability of extensions, and to make sure they
are implemented properly in a common way. There are very few extensions to the
fnmatch pattern format, since POSIX is very strict unlike regular expressions,
but POSIX does allow a few extensions like `[^` behaving the same as `[^`.

Overall this test suite is very comprehensive and should provide a near
exhaustive coverage of the fnmatch features. I read every relevant sentence in
POSIX and made sure a test case covered each sentence.
